      text: "Ask people what they regret, and you might expect a list of dramatic mistakes: the wrong marriage, the disastrous investment, the angry email sent at midnight. Yet research on regret paints a quieter and perhaps more surprising picture. In one large international survey, thousands of people were asked to describe their biggest regret. Their answers fell into a few broad groups. Some regretted not building a solid foundation for their lives: \"I wish I had saved more money,\" \"If only I had looked after my health.\" Others regretted moral failures, times when they had treated someone unfairly. But two groups stood out. The first were regrets of connection: friendships they had let fade, relatives they had lost touch with, apologies they had never made. The second were regrets of boldness: the business they never started, the trip they never took, the person they never asked out. Psychologists have noticed a clear pattern over time. In the short term, we tend to regret things we did: the reckless purchase, the foolish comment. Over the years, however, these regrets fade, partly because we can usually explain them, apologise or make amends. What lasts are the regrets of inaction. \"I wish I had tried\" hurts longer than \"I wish I hadn't\", perhaps because we can never know how things might have turned out. A palliative care nurse who spent years with patients in their final weeks described something similar. Very few of the people she cared for wished they had worked harder. Many wished they had stayed in touch with old friends, or had found the courage to live the life they wanted rather than the life others expected of them. Should we, then, try to live without regret? Most experts say no. Regret, unpleasant as it is, can be useful, because it tells us what we value. The key is not to dwell on the past endlessly but to use regret as a guide. If you find yourself thinking \"If only I had called her more often,\" the lesson is not to torture yourself but to pick up the phone today. In retrospect, the things we regret most are rarely the risks we took. They are the chances we let pass while telling ourselves there would always be another one.",
